Ok, if I understood correctly we should still return `MAX_WAIT` in situations where the next timeout is longer than we can represent (`INT_MAX`). The problem with the current code is that when `CONFIG_SYSTEM_CLOCK_SLOPPY_IDLE` is set, `MAX_WAIT` will be `K_TICKS_FOREVER` that is a negative number so `CLAMP` will end up always returning `MAX_WAIT`

```
CLAMP(val, low, high) (((val) <= (low)) ? (low) : MIN(val, high))
```

because in `MIN(val, high)`  the `high` value is a negative number. I think it can be solved in the following way:

```
	if ((to == NULL) ||
	    ((int64_t)(to->dticks - ticks_elapsed) > (int64_t)INT_MAX)) {
		ret = MAX_WAIT;
	} else {
		ret = MAX(0, to->dticks - ticks_elapsed);
	}
```
